["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Turns Software From an Engineering Constraint Into a Capital One Martin Casado and Steven Sinofsky&#x27;s central claim: AI has moved software from an engineering constraint to a capital constraint — a team of 20 can now effectively spend a billion dollars, which is why startups can compete head-on with the incumbents. The a16z Show a16z The New Economics of AI | Martin Casado &amp; Steven Sinofsky Compute Capital Startups Venture Capital AI Economics Incumbent Competition Foom / fast takeoff TAM / Total Addressable Market in-distribution Mythical man-month expert systems\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-a16z-ai-正在把工程问题变成资本问题/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:03:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>AI Turns Software From an Engineering Constraint Into a Capital One</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Martin Casado and Steven Sinofsky&#x27;s central claim: AI has moved software from an engineering constraint to a capital constraint — a team of 20 can now effectively spend a billion dollars, which is why startups can compete head-on with the incumbents.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Compute</span><span class=\"tag\">Capital</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Administrative Humiliation Is Why the Poor Hate Government: Applying for Benefits Is a Time Tax Administrative burden in government benefit programs is not an efficiency problem but an instrument of social control: it punishes the poor with humiliation and paperwork, turns progressive policy into a funnel that screens out all but a few, and ends by making recipients hate the government. The Ezra Klein Show Ezra Klein Show This Is Why People Hate the Government Welfare state Administrative burden Time tax Medicaid Tax reform American politics time tax ordeal mechanism improper payment only-once policy\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-ezra-时间税-行政羞辱是穷人恨政府的原因/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a8482192037a6711-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:17:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Ezra Klein Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Administrative Humiliation Is Why the Poor Hate Government: Applying for Benefits Is a Time Tax</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Administrative burden in government benefit programs is not an efficiency problem but an instrument of social control: it punishes the poor with humiliation and paperwork, turns progressive policy into a funnel that screens out all but a few, and ends by making recipients hate the government.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Welfare state</span><span class=\"tag\">Administrative burden</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Human Click Data Is a Bug: Agent Search Has to Rebuild Indexing and Pricing When search&#x27;s customer stops being a human and becomes an agent, click data stops working, and the index, the pricing and the business model all have to be built again; Parag offers a new framework built on a quality/cost/latency triangle and on Shapley values. Training Data Training Data Parallel’s Parag Agrawal: Building a New Web for AI Agents Agent search Web economics Shapley value Google Cloud Data pricing Infrastructure Shapley value grounding fresh index memory hierarchy\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-trainingdata-人类点击数据是-bug-agent-搜索重建网络经济/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bca134f7cdc5e5aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">55:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Training Data</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Human Click Data Is a Bug: Agent Search Has to Rebuild Indexing and Pricing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When search&#x27;s customer stops being a human and becomes an agent, click data stops working, and the index, the pricing and the business model all have to be built again; Parag offers a new framework built on a quality/cost/latency triangle and on Shapley values.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agent search</span><span class=\"tag\">Web economics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Background agents will eat 90% of compute, so inference should trade latency for throughput The next wave of AI inference is not chat but background agents, and they will eat 90% of compute. Sale Research therefore optimizes for throughput rather than latency, and will even accept 80% availability, in exchange for prices that fall by orders of magnitude — and it argues Nvidia&#x27;s performance-per-watt advantage is overrated. Invest Like the Best Invest Like the Best Neil Movva - Making AI 10x Cheaper - [Invest Like the Best, EP.488] AI Inference GPUs Data Centers Open Models Agents Compute Cost KV cache HBM (high-bandwidth memory) SRAM (static random-access memory) Tensor Core Harness\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-ilt-推理成本需降3-6个数量级-后台任务才是主战场/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/32e8a839c878b0c2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Invest Like the Best</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Background agents will eat 90% of compute, so inference should trade latency for throughput</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The next wave of AI inference is not chat but background agents, and they will eat 90% of compute. Sale Research therefore optimizes for throughput rather than latency, and will even accept 80% availability, in exchange for prices that fall by orders of magnitude — and it argues Nvidia&#x27;s performance-per-watt advantage is overrated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Inference</span><span class=\"tag\">GPUs</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"There Is No Formula for Getting In: The Anti-Anxiety Truth About U.S. College Admissions Low admit rates are a story about roughly 80 selective schools nationally; for most families there is no formula, and the most useful thing a parent can do is hold a standing meeting and control what is controllable. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 286: ENCORE: Getting into College with Rick Clark US college admissions Admit rates Application essays Test-optional Athletic scholarships Parent-teen relationship holistic review test-optional Common App FAFSA net price calculator\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-asklisa-申请没有万能配方-美国大学录取的反焦虑真相/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">48:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>There Is No Formula for Getting In: The Anti-Anxiety Truth About U.S. College Admissions</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Low admit rates are a story about roughly 80 selective schools nationally; for most families there is no formula, and the most useful thing a parent can do is hold a standing meeting and control what is controllable.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">US college admissions</span><span class=\"tag\">Admit rates</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"No Atrocities in the SS Officer&#x27;s Album — Only Blueberries and Holidays A private photo album kept by an SS officer records not atrocities but holidays, dinners and blueberry picking; it proves the men who carried out the killing knew exactly what they were doing, and it is a reminder that understanding the Holocaust means looking at the perpetrators. Witness History Witness History Auschwitz: An SS officer’s photo album Holocaust Nazis Photo album Perpetrator studies Auschwitz Historical evidence Solahütte Counterintelligence Corps Frankfurt Auschwitz Trials\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-witness-纳粹屠夫的私人相册-没有惨状只有蓝莓和假期/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>No Atrocities in the SS Officer&#x27;s Album — Only Blueberries and Holidays</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A private photo album kept by an SS officer records not atrocities but holidays, dinners and blueberry picking; it proves the men who carried out the killing knew exactly what they were doing, and it is a reminder that understanding the Holocaust means looking at the perpetrators.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Holocaust</span><span class=\"tag\">Nazis</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Twenty Thousand Robots Shipped, Nine in Ten Idle: Embodied AI Enters Its ROI Era Beneath the heat of WRC, there may be only one or two startups genuinely doing pre-training, real deployment demand may be only 10% of shipments, and the industry has moved on from demo theater into a hands-on phase where ROI has to be calculated. 卫诗婕｜漫谈Light the Star 漫谈Light the Star 84.49 家央企采买进场，史上最热WRC：泡沫被挤压，具身行业进入实战阶段｜14 年从业者的行家鉴别指南 Embodied AI Humanoid Robots WRC World Models Data Pipeline Robotics Investment VLA / Vision-Language-Action model World Model Teleoperation EMG / Electromyography signals Data Pipeline Post-training\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-lightstar-两万台出货九成吃灰-具身行业进入算roi的实战期/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/adbddf5c93cfc63c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"卫\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">卫诗婕｜漫谈Light the Star</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Twenty Thousand Robots Shipped, Nine in Ten Idle: Embodied AI Enters Its ROI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Beneath the heat of WRC, there may be only one or two startups genuinely doing pre-training, real deployment demand may be only 10% of shipments, and the industry has moved on from demo theater into a hands-on phase where ROI has to be calculated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Humanoid Robots</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Intel Drops EMIB for UCIe: 1.2GB of Cache Behind 256 Cores Diamond Rapids is expected in 2027 with up to 256 P-cores, 1.2GB of last-level cache and 16 channels of DDR5; it replaces EMIB with trimmable compute building blocks and a UCIe interconnect, and the huge cache is its answer to the AI era. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato How Intel&#x27;s New CPU Finally Makes Sense CPU architecture Data centers Intel Xeon Chiplets Last-level cache AI infrastructure Fan-out fabric architecture Compute building block Fabric hub UCIe Foveros 3D Direct\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-techtechpotato-下一代-xeon-把宝押在-1-2gb-末级缓存上/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fc7c90dc34e0b3f0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">21:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Intel Drops EMIB for UCIe: 1.2GB of Cache Behind 256 Cores</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Diamond Rapids is expected in 2027 with up to 256 P-cores, 1.2GB of last-level cache and 16 channels of DDR5; it replaces EMIB with trimmable compute building blocks and a UCIe interconnect, and the huge cache is its answer to the AI era.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">CPU architecture</span><span class=\"tag\">Data centers</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"95% of AI Agent Projects Die in Pilot: What&#x27;s Missing Isn&#x27;t Intelligence, It&#x27;s a Control Layer AI has moved from generating content to acting on its own. 95% of agent projects are stuck in pilot and never reach production, because what&#x27;s missing isn&#x27;t intelligence but a runtime control layer. Eye On A.I. Eye On A.I. 95% of AI Agent Projects Fail to Reach Production. Here&#x27;s Why | Manoj Saxena, TrustWise Agents AI governance Enterprise software Runtime control Token cost runtime governance AI control tower guardian agents semantic action layer trust posture management\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-eyeonai-智能体流量首超人类-ai-失控才是落地最大瓶颈/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b277eb062496b5a5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Eye On A.I.</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>95% of AI Agent Projects Die in Pilot: What&#x27;s Missing Isn&#x27;t Intelligence, It&#x27;s a Control Layer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI has moved from generating content to acting on its own. 95% of agent projects are stuck in pilot and never reach production, because what&#x27;s missing isn&#x27;t intelligence but a runtime control layer.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agents</span><span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The Solid State Transformer Has Been Hyped for Fifty Years; AI Data Centers Are Its First Real Buyer The solid state transformer failed three times over — in the navy, in high-speed rail, in the smart grid — because all it offered was ‘smaller’. The copper problem and the 800-volt DC problem inside AI data centers are the first reasons it has ever had to exist. Asianometry Asianometry Has the Solid State Transformer’s Time Finally Come? Power electronics Data center power Solid state transformers 800V DC Silicon carbide Startup funding Solid State Transformer (SST) IGBT Silicon Carbide (SiC) Galvanic isolation 800VDC architecture PETT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-asianometry-固态变压器吹了五十年-ai数据中心是第一个真买家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a4d5049243d5f237-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Solid State Transformer Has Been Hyped for Fifty Years; AI Data Centers Are Its First Real Buyer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The solid state transformer failed three times over — in the navy, in high-speed rail, in the smart grid — because all it offered was ‘smaller’. The copper problem and the 800-volt DC problem inside AI data centers are the first reasons it has ever had to exist.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Power electronics</span><span class=\"tag\">Data center power</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"A Pitch Tells Two Stories: The Project, And The Buyer&#x27;s Promotion Hollywood screenwriter Rhett Reese on what a pitch actually is: the project story runs on the surface, the story of the other person&#x27;s success runs underneath. Make them the protagonist, lower their risk, enlarge their reward, and your pitch lands far more often. Think Fast Talk Smart Think Fast Talk Smart 317. Plot Twist: The Second Story Hiding Inside Every Great Pitch Pitching Communication Hollywood AI Collaboration pitch gatekeeper auteur\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-thinkfast-好提案藏着两个故事-你的故事和对方升职的故事/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7fd7a03d438b4365-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">24:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Think Fast Talk Smart</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>A Pitch Tells Two Stories: The Project, And The Buyer&#x27;s Promotion</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Hollywood screenwriter Rhett Reese on what a pitch actually is: the project story runs on the surface, the story of the other person&#x27;s success runs underneath. Make them the protagonist, lower their risk, enlarge their reward, and your pitch lands far more often.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Pitching</span><span class=\"tag\">Communication</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Black Holes Have Only Two Parameters, but How They Form Is Still a Mystery Gravitational waves and the EHT confirm that black holes really do have only two parameters, but how a 120-solar-mass black hole forms, whether dark matter is made of tiny black holes, and how the last parsec gets crossed all remain unsolved. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 365 | Vitor Cardoso on Why Black Holes Are Special Black holes Gravitational waves Event Horizon Telescope Dark matter Quantum gravity LISA no-hair theorem Kerr metric light ring cosmic censorship matched filtering last parsec problem\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-mindscape-黑洞只有两个参数-形成机制却仍是谜/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:05:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Black Holes Have Only Two Parameters, but How They Form Is Still a Mystery</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Gravitational waves and the EHT confirm that black holes really do have only two parameters, but how a 120-solar-mass black hole forms, whether dark matter is made of tiny black holes, and how the last parsec gets crossed all remain unsolved.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Black holes</span><span class=\"tag\">Gravitational waves</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves. EconTalk EconTalk Are We Alone? Cosmic Loneliness and the Longing to Believe (with Adam Kirsch) UFOs Extraterrestrial life Philosophy of science Anti-expert sentiment Social psychology UAP euphemism treadmill book experience Drake equation AATIP\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-econtalk-所有外星人想象都是人类的自画像/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:11:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">UFOs</span><span class=\"tag\">Extraterrestrial life</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area Kidneys for sale Kidney Transplants Organ Sales Market Design Moral Controversy Public Policy repugnant transaction market design non-directed donor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-grayarea-禁止卖肾的代价-本不该失去的生命/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">49:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Kidney Transplants</span><span class=\"tag\">Organ Sales</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab How to Improve Motivation &amp; Overcome Procrastination | Dr. Masud Husain Motivation Procrastination Dopamine Neuroscience Alzheimer&#x27;s disease Addiction apathy basal ganglia activation energy wanting vs liking\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-huberman-动机不是欲望问题-而是激活能量障碍/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e03163dcfb779e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:20:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Motivation</span><span class=\"tag\">Procrastination</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid. Odd Lots Odd Lots The Nigerian Industrial Behemoth That Could Reshape the African Economy African economy industrial policy manufacturing demographics China-Africa relations land reform flying geese paradigm resource curse developmental state kulak demonstration effect\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-oddlots-非洲工业化不缺资源-缺的是人口密度/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">58:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">African economy</span><span class=\"tag\">industrial policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city. Witness History Witness History Uruguay&#x27;s water referendum Water rights Referendum Privatisation Uruguay Social movements Constitution concession mate\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-witness-供水私有化不是宿命-乌拉圭公投的水权胜利/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Water rights</span><span class=\"tag\">Referendum</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news. This American Life This American Life 206: Somewhere in the Arabian Sea U.S. military aircraft carrier daily life at war organizational management portraits of the young 9/11 blood chit Tailhook scandal Hollywood shower berthing XO / executive officer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-tal-航母战争的日常-补零食-防谈恋爱-等回家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1da611bfe52eccd0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">This American Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">U.S. military</span><span class=\"tag\">aircraft carrier</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Tech Making Our Kids Less Capable? With Dr Jared Cooney Horvath Ed tech Classroom screens Cognitive development AI in education Digital literacy Contrarian Ed tech ICILS Offloading Outsourcing SRLs Drill-and-kill\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-iq2-技术不等于学习-屏幕正在让一代人变笨/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/46f8e9d9c37f6c72-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">33:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ed tech</span><span class=\"tag\">Classroom screens</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 699. The Troubles: Return of the IRA (Part 3) The Troubles IRA Counter-insurgency British politics Insurgency Sectarian violence The Troubles Operation Demetrius UVF\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-restishistory-临时ira绝非防御组织-英军误判铸成三十年冲突/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:51</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">IRA</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 398: Donald Davidson on Mind vs. Body (Part Two) Mind-body problem Philosophy of action Anomalous monism Causal explanation Davidson Analytic philosophy token identity homonomic generalization heteronomic generalization pro attitude primary reason rationalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-pel-心物无规律-理由仍可作为原因而自由/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4024c3bbaf655b13-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">53:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mind-body problem</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy of action</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI. 十字路口Crossing 十字路口Crossing 22 岁的具身 CEO、5 轮融资、过亿美元、“不知天有多高”、“一年吃了十年的苦”｜对谈黄一：萝博派对创始人/CEO Embodied AI Robotics Fundraising Open Source Startup Management Org Design missing layer deep pocket BFM (behavior foundation model) sim2real honeycomb structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-crossing-具身创业最优解-快融资-软条款-全栈开源/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3434775ccb19b8d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"十\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">十字路口Crossing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Robotics Fundraising</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato The Reason My Phone is Ringing Daily: Etched AI chips Transformer-specific chips Compute efficiency Funding Bandwidth bottleneck tape out RDMA threshold voltage cluster scale memory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-techtechpotato-etched的芯片-可能解决的是带宽而不是算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5ab75e84ff855e23-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Transformer-specific chips</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code. Peter Yang Peter Yang How to Build Better AI Evals with Claude Code in 5 Steps | Shreya &amp; Hamel Eval systems Claude Code Error analysis LLM products Human review bottom-up evals top-down evals LLM judge error discovery staccato fragments slop\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-peteryang-自下而上的评估-是-ai-给不了你的护城河/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/49510d80b4317525-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">54:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Peter Yang</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Eval systems</span><span class=\"tag\">Claude Code</span>\n</div></div></a>"]
